If a transaction does not work in an inner method in Spring Framework / Spring Boot,
 the most common reason is Spring’s proxy-based transaction management.

1️⃣ The Problem (Self Invocation)

Spring applies @Transactional using proxies.
If a method inside a class calls another method of the same class, the call bypasses the proxy, so the transaction is not applied.

Example (Transaction will NOT work)
@Service
public class OrderService {

    public void createOrder() {
        saveOrder();   // internal call
    }

    @Transactional
    public void saveOrder() {
        // transaction expected here but may not start
    }
}

Why?

Client → Proxy → createOrder() → saveOrder()
                     ↑
               direct call (no proxy)

Since saveOrder() is called internally, it does not pass through the proxy, so @Transactional is ignored.

2️⃣ Solution 1 (Recommended)

Move the transactional method to another service class.

@Service
public class OrderService {

    @Autowired
    private PaymentService paymentService;

    public void createOrder() {
        paymentService.saveOrder();
    }
}
@Service
public class PaymentService {

    @Transactional
    public void saveOrder() {
        // transaction works here
    }
}

Now the call goes:

Client → Proxy(OrderService) → Proxy(PaymentService) → saveOrder()

So transaction works.

3️⃣ Solution 2 (Call Through Proxy)

Inject the same bean.

@Autowired
private OrderService self;

public void createOrder() {
    self.saveOrder(); // goes through proxy
}
4️⃣ Other Reasons Transactions May Not Work

Check these too:

Method is private (@Transactional works only on public methods).

Class is not a Spring bean (@Service, @Component missing).

Exception not thrown (transaction rolls back only on runtime exceptions by default).

Wrong propagation setting.

5️⃣ Interview One-Line Answer

In Spring, @Transactional does not work on inner method calls because Spring uses proxy-based AOP, and self-invocation bypasses the proxy,
 so the transactional advice is not applied.